<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Akwesasne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as St. Regis Mohawk Reservation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Akwesasne is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> community on the St Lawrence river that straddles the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, divided among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> State. It grew from a Catholic mission settlement founded in the mid-18th century by Mohawks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Kahnawake</a>. With some 12,000 residents it is one of the largest Mohawk communities, and the international border running through it has made it a site of disputes over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and smuggling.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/akwesasne.jpg" alt="Akwesasne" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Doug Kerr · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akwesasne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The territory takes in part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence River</a>, the mouths of the Raquette and St. Regis rivers and many islands in all three. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> border divides it north from south, and the boundary between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> divides the northern part. Its main areas are Kawehno:ke, or Cornwall Island, in Ontario, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kana">Kana</a>:takon (Saint Regis) and Tsi Snaihne (Snye) in Quebec. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> portion is the federally recognised St. Regis Mohawk Reservation. In Mohawk, Ahkwesáhsne means Land Where the Partridge Drums, after the area's rich wildlife.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the time of its founding the community was counted among the Seven Nations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, and the settlement of the border in the early 19th century placed the larger part of it within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Its Catholic mission survives, with a large stone church dating to 1795, and parish records show that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> recorded residents' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> names through the 18th and 19th centuries. In 1888 the superintendent of Indian Affairs obtained the surrender of Dundee, land leased to settlers, which the community always contested; in December 2018 it accepted a settlement of $240 million, 37 years after filing its claim.</p><p>By the 20th century Akwesasne had renewed its ties with the Haudenosaunee Confederacy. Its residents have repeatedly asserted the right of free passage across the border granted to Indigenous peoples by the Jay Treaty of 1795: in 1969 they blocked the bridge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> over duties on personal purchases, and in June 2009 a protest against the arming of Canadian border officers led the officers to leave the Cornwall port of entry. Disputes over the casino also divided the community, and in 1989 firebombs were thrown at a chartered bus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Three bodies govern the territory: the traditional Mohawk Nation Council of Chiefs, or Longhouse, a member of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy; the elected Mohawk Council of Akwesasne on the Canadian side; and the Saint Regis Mohawk Tribe on the American side. The residents regard themselves as one community but keep separate police forces. In 2016 Akwesasne set up its own court for non-criminal offences, which relies on restorative justice.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Akwesasne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mohawk communities today include <b>Akwesasne</b>, Kahnawake and Kanesatake, scene of the 1990 Oka crisis, along with Six Nations and Tyendinaga in Ontario.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohawk-nation">Mohawk Nation</a></li></ul></section>
